"1. Load the three raw files from the working directory: u.data (MovieLens100K), VideoGames.csv (Amazon Video Games), and user_taggedartists-timestamps.dat (Last.FM).\n2. Parse each dataset into a LensKit 0.14.4 feedback dataset with correct user-item-interaction schema and timestamps/ratings handled appropriately.\n3. Apply 5-core filtering to each dataset before any splitting; remove users/items iteratively until every remaining user and item has at least 5 interactions.\n4. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert ratings > 3 to implicit positive interactions and discard ratings <= 3.\n5. Keep Last.FM as implicit feedback without rating-threshold conversion, unless the raw file format requires mapping tags/events to positive interactions.\n6. Generate exactly 5 reproducible random data-splitting seeds and use each seed consistently across all datasets and algorithms.\n7. For every dataset-seed combination, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split that places each user\u2019s interactions into train/test while preserving user coverage in the test protocol as required by LensKit.\n8. Train three algorithms in LensKit 0.14.4: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop.\n9. Use standard/default hyperparameters for all algorithms, with no tuning on test data.\n10. Fit each model only on the training portion of each split and generate top-N recommendations for held-out users.\n11. Evaluate recommendations with nDCG@k and Precision@k for k = 1, 5, and 10.\n12. Ensure metric computation uses only the held-out test interactions and handles users with no test items according to the evaluation protocol.\n13. Aggregate results across the 5 seeds separately for each dataset and algorithm, reporting mean and variability (e.g., standard deviation or confidence intervals).\n14. Run a short statistical analysis of seed sensitivity, comparing performance variation across the 5 random splits for each dataset-algorithm-metric combination.\n15. Produce outputs that clearly identify dataset, algorithm, seed, and metric values so the random-seed effect on accuracy can be assessed."